[size=4][u]Station Architect[/u][/size]

Tell the build menu what your station should produce or build: this extension works out every module needed and places them as one compact block. Nothing is bought until you press [b]Confirm[/b].

[size=3][u][b]Features[/b][/u][/size]
[list]
[*] [b]Target planner[/b]: say what the station should make, not which modules: wares on the [b]Wares[/b] tab, ships to build or resupply per hour on the [b]Ships[/b] tab; several targets in one plan.
[*] [b]Compact layout[/b]: production, storage and habitats packed tight on connection modules; build modules on the sides with clear approach lanes; docks and piers outward.
[*] [b]Full cycle and workforce[/b]: optionally every intermediate ware down to raw resources and energy, habitats for the workforce, and its food and medicine.
[*] [b]Read from the game[/b]: production rates, sunlight and workforce bonuses, module sizes, docking bays, ship build times and the production lines of every build module; docks sized for the planned traffic.
[*] [b]Docking[/b]: the dock areas and piers the planned trade needs, each count on a slider you can lower.
[*] [b]Special resource modules[/b]: Scrap Processors fed by tugs; with [b]Full cycle[/b] their scrap metal goes into your target wares and your ships' wares before any regular production.
[*] [b]Preview first[/b]: modules, each ware's balance per hour, workforce and warnings before anything is placed.
[*] [b]Existing stations[/b]: reads what a station makes and fills any shortfall, its docks included.
[*] [b]From the map[/b]: right-click empty space, [b]Plan a station here[/b], and the build menu opens with the run started.
[*] [b]Bill route[/b]: a manual list of modules and counts, placed the same way.
[*] [b]Plot licence[/b]: shows the plot size and price where the plot costs money; [b]Optimize plot[/b] shrinks it afterwards.
[*] [b]The plan stays yours[/b]: edit, save or load it by hand; [b]Confirm module changes[/b] pays as usual, [b]Cancel module changes[/b] discards it.
[/list]
[size=3][u][b]Limitations[/b][/u][/size]
[list]
[*] Works only inside the open build menu of a station you own, with your own blueprints.
[*] This mod requires [b]Protected UI Mode[/b] in the game's Extensions settings to be disabled.
[*] Windows version of the game only: the placement route reads the game's own editor in memory, which the native Linux build does not expose the same way. The Windows game running on Linux through Proton or Wine should work.
[*] Verified on the 8.00 and 9.00 builds of the game. On a build it does not recognise, the panel says the editor is not reachable, or that the Extra Data API cannot read module data, and Run stays off; nothing else in the build menu changes.
[*] The plot is limited to the game's 20 km: a layout that needs more, docks and piers included, is refused before anything is placed. When the docks and piers are what does not fit, lower the [b]Docking[/b] sliders.
[*] Extralarge modules are never planned. Vanilla has none among its blueprints; some total conversions add station-sized ones, and the plan takes the smaller modules instead.
[*] Ship build times assume the station has its build drones; a shipyard without them builds several times slower than planned.
[*] Docks and storage are sized for a target's whole output, as if all of it sells. A module far above vanilla rates (some modded mining modules make millions of units per hour) can then bring dozens of piers, dock areas and storage modules when its ware is a target, an existing station's starting target included: pick a smaller kind of module in the ware list where it offers one, or lower the [b]Docking[/b] sliders and [b]Storage, hours of flow[/b] to the trade you expect.
[*] Such a module that only feeds the production chain runs part-time and gets a warning, but [b]Workforce[/b] still houses all its workers; if a smaller variant is among your blueprints, the plan takes that one.
[*] A module the game's editor refuses is never dropped silently: the placement starts over once, and a second refusal removes every module placed.
[/list]
