[size=3][u][b]Installation[/b][/u][/size]

You can download the latest version via Steam client - [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3827769999]Station Architect[/url]
Or you can do it via the Nexus Mods - [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/x4foundations/mods/2520]Station Architect[/url]

[size=3][u][b]Save state[/b][/u][/size]

[b]No[/b] (removing the extension does not break saves).

[size=3][u][b]Usage[/b][/u][/size]

Open the build menu of one of your stations. The Station Architect panel sits under the menu's title bar; expand it to plan. It has three tabs: [b]Wares[/b], [b]Ships[/b] and [b]Bill[/b]. Wares and Ships edit one target, so one plan can make wares and build ships; the special resource modules, the options and the docking under them are shared. What a station has counts its built modules and those already planned in the open menu; expanding the panel reads them again.

[size=3][u][i]Wares[/i][/u][/size]
[list=1]
[*] Pick a ware and press [b]Add a target[/b]. Each added target gets a slider for its number of production modules, with the ware's net per hour now and planned; on an existing station a slider starts at what the station already has, and releasing it at that lowest value removes the target. Modules with the same production count as one kind. A ware your blueprints make with several kinds (another race's recipe, a faster modded module) is listed once per kind, as "Ware / Module", and each such target counts only its own kind; with a [b]Modules race[/b] set, the list keeps that race's kinds.
[*] Set the options:
[/list][list][list]
[*] [b]Modules race[/b]: the race whose modules and ship hulls the plan prefers; [b]Any[/b] chooses from all your blueprints. Habitats, and under [b]Self-sufficient workforce[/b] the modules that feed them, follow [b]Workforce race[/b]. A new station's connection structures follow [b]Modules race[/b]; under [b]Any[/b], the race holding most of the planned habitats and production modules, or your smallest connection structures when those modules are all generic.
[*] [b]Full cycle[/b]: every intermediate ware down to raw resources and energy.
[*] [b]Workforce[/b]: habitats for the workforce of the planned modules. On an existing station it starts unticked while the station has no habitat.
[*] [b]Self-sufficient workforce[/b]: with [b]Full cycle[/b] and [b]Workforce[/b], the food and medicine of that workforce too.
[*] [b]Workforce race[/b]: the race of the habitats' workforce, and so of what it eats. The list holds the races you have met.
[*] [b]Storage, hours of flow[/b]: how many hours of each ware's flow the storage holds, 1 to 24, 4 by default. Each ware gets one module of the smallest size that holds it, several only of the largest size.
[/list]
[/list]3. [b]Docking[/b] has one slider for dock areas and one for piers, from what the station has up to what the planned trade needs. Both start at the top; a lowered one scales along when the target changes, and back at the top it follows the need again. On an existing station they show without a target too, sized for the station's own trade.
[list=1][*] Check the preview: the planned modules, each ware's net per hour now and planned, the workforce and trade line, and any warning. On an existing station a line under the planned modules names the shortfalls the plan covers: wares the station makes that run short (under [b]Full cycle[/b] also the wares it uses) and missing housing.
[/list]
[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-StationArchitect/refs/heads/main/docs/images/panel_target.png[/img]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-StationArchitect/refs/heads/main/docs/images/panel_existing.png[/img]

[size=3][u][i]Ships[/i][/u][/size]

Pick a build module from your blueprints, a fabrication bay or a maintenance bay, and press [b]Add a module[/b]. Every build module kind the station has or you added gets a slider of its count, starting at what the station has; an added kind released at 0 is removed.
[list]
[*] Under a fabrication bay, one slider per ship size it builds sets the [b]ships per hour[/b], up to what those bays can build. It starts at that top, the bays' full capacity: lower it to the ships you expect to sell. An existing yard's bays start there too, so its first plan sizes docks and storage for full capacity. Sizes that share a bay share its time, so lowering one raises the other's top.
[*] Under a maintenance bay, one slider per ship size sets the [b]resupplies per hour[/b] (restocking a ship's ammunition and units). It starts at a tenth of what the bays manage.
[*] [b]now[/b] on a slider is what the station's bays are working on at the moment, per hour.
[/list]
Ships are planned with the game's High loadout, which sets their build time and wares; a bay builds one ship per production line at a time. The plan adds the wares those ships and resupplies use, their production chain under [b]Full cycle[/b], and the storage and docks for that flow. The special resource modules, the options and the Docking sliders are the ones of the Wares tab.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-StationArchitect/refs/heads/main/docs/images/panel_ships.png[/img]

[size=3][u][i]Special resource modules[/i][/u][/size]

Both tabs have this section. It works with [b]Full cycle[/b] only and is greyed out without it. Special resource modules (Scrap Processors) are fed by tugs rather than by trade. Pick one and press [b]Add a module[/b]: its slider sets how many the station has, from its own count up to 10 more, and shows the module's output per hour; released at the station's count it is removed. On an existing station the section starts with the modules it has.

Their scrap metal feeds the scrap recycling modules that make your target wares and your ships' wares; only the rest comes from regular production modules, and a surplus is sold. Tugs bring the raw scrap straight to them, so it adds no docks, piers or storage.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-StationArchitect/refs/heads/main/docs/images/panel_special.png[/img]

[size=3][u][i]Run[/i][/u][/size]
[list=1]
[*] Press [b]Run[/b]. The run first finds a layout. Where the plot costs money, it then shows the plot size it needs and the licence price, and places nothing until you press [b]Buy licence[/b]; [b]Cancel[/b] ends the run.
[/list]
[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-StationArchitect/refs/heads/main/docs/images/panel_licence.png[/img]
[list=1]
[*] The run places the modules one after another, in turns from the bottom up: the station works after its first turn and every turn adds output, its storage and production chain from energy up, the final product last, then the dock areas and piers its trade needs. A new station opens its first turn with one pier and one dock area and grows from that pier; habitats and the modules that only feed them come after 40 % of the turns, spread evenly over the rest. The panel shows the progress and the turn, and [b]Stop[/b] halts the run after the current module. Changing the plan by hand stops the run too, and closing the build menu ends it. The menu's lists are rebuilt once when the run ends, not after every module.
[/list]
[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-StationArchitect/refs/heads/main/docs/images/panel_running.png[/img]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-StationArchitect/refs/heads/main/docs/images/build_menu_run.png[/img]
[list=1]
[*] If the game's editor still refuses a module after a few retries, the run removes every module it placed and starts the placement over, once. A second refusal removes everything again, and the folded panel names the module.
[*] When the run ends the panel folds to its result (placed, failed). Check the plan, change what you like by hand, and press [b]Confirm module changes[/b] as usual. [b]Optimize plot[/b] fits the plot to the planned modules, so a plot the run grew is not paid larger than needed. [b]Reset[/b] starts the target over from what the station has. While a plot's licence is still due (a new plot before its run, or after a [b]Bill[/b] run, which grows the plot without asking), the panel shows the fee with its own [b]Buy licence[/b].
[/list]
[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-StationArchitect/refs/heads/main/docs/images/build_menu_done.png[/img]

[size=3][u][i]From the map[/i][/u][/size]

Right-click empty space in a sector on the map and choose [b]Plan a station here[/b].

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-StationArchitect/refs/heads/main/docs/images/map_context_menu.png[/img]

The dialog has the same [b]Wares[/b] and [b]Ships[/b] tabs, plus [b]Preset for all station modules[/b] (the loadout of the new modules).

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-StationArchitect/refs/heads/main/docs/images/map_dialog.png[/img]

OK closes the dialog and puts the game's own plot box on your cursor, as the Plots tab's [b]Create plot[/b] does: the box turns red where a plot may not go, and a left click reserves it. A right click or Escape before that click cancels the plan.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-StationArchitect/refs/heads/main/docs/images/map_plot_placement.png[/img]

The build menu then opens and the run starts. The plot starts at 1 km and grows with the modules; [b]Confirm module changes[/b] stays your click.

[size=3][u][i]Bill[/i][/u][/size]

The manual route: [b]Add[/b] puts a row in the bill, with the category of the row above; pick its category and a module from your blueprints and type the count, 1 to 99. Repeat for every module type; a row's X removes it, [b]Clear[/b] empties the bill.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-StationArchitect/refs/heads/main/docs/images/panel_bill.png[/img]

[b]Run[/b] places the modules on the station's free connections, nearest to the centre first, docks, piers, build and defence modules on outward connections. Connection modules in the bill are not placed as such: they are the budget of connectors the run may insert when a module finds no free connection.

[size=3][u][b]Options[/b][/u][/size]

In the game's Extension Options, the Station Architect page has a Debug Level setting (None, Debug, Trace) for the game log.

